<a id="обновление-общей-поставки"></a>

# Update the global Delivery

If one user message accepts and activates an already shown exact ready result, apply the [combined decision](commit.md#user-decision) without asking again. Ordinary installation/trial activation does not imply project acceptance or replace human observation.

Work only on an explicit High Grade update request. Select a concrete source. Do not search latest or scan projects. For Git, record the selected SHA, create an isolated archive/worktree copy and build its CLI under the [installation reference](../references/tools/installation.md). A dirty tree is not a candidate. Using it requires an explicitly identified scope and immutable snapshot, without an automatic commit. For a package, record origin/fingerprints and use its shipped CLI. Matching binary version alone does not prove origin. Clarify only an ambiguous source choice.

Before switching, check current installation with its CLI. Preserve a known-working copy outside release directories for failure diagnosis. On status failure, follow recovery below. Record the previous release ID. Check candidate manifest, hashes, CLI version, skill ownership under the active journal and common-rule changes. Run preview with candidate CLI. A valid preview normally returns status=unknown, exit 2 and SemanticReviewRequired with validation=passed, decision=required, applied=false. This requests semantic review; it is neither installation failure nor successful apply. Save candidate_sha256. After review, use --apply true --candidate-sha256 with that same fingerprint. Diagnose other errors/unknown findings from actual results. A candidate change requires another preview. After apply, verify global-status; error/unknown is not success. Release materials are immutable. After successful postcheck, CLI removes its owned inactive-release files and preserves foreign content with a warning. Failure recovery is not a permanent rollback option after successful update.

<a id="повтор-и-ошибки"></a>

## Retry and failures

- GlobalSameRelease forbids switching again but does not prove equal content. After successful global-status, compare candidate manifest.json SHA256 with manifest_sha256 from the verified profile/.highgrade/global/releases/<active>/journal.json, and candidate CLI SHA256 with installed CLI (`highgrade` on Linux, `highgrade.exe` on Windows). manifest.json itself is not installed. Its verified hash binds materials; status checks integrity. Only matching hashes justify already installed, without apply. A mismatch is an immutable-release conflict. Do not overwrite or rename to bypass it; prepare a separate new release. An unavailable comparison stays unknown.
- On failure, preserve the original error and immediately run global-status with the preserved former CLI. During Approve → Commit migration, it may not understand new journal fields/content. Diagnose this and, for GlobalSkillRecoveryRequired, recover with the same verified candidate CLI. An old-format rejection alone does not prove damage. Postcheck recovery exists but can also fail. Report the actually confirmed active release and unfinished cleanup separately from update failure. If status fails, state is unconfirmed. Stop further installation changes except the explicit recovery below. For other errors, propose cause-based recovery. Never edit the pointer or clean blindly. Instructions alone do not prove successful recovery.
- GlobalSkillRecoveryRequired identifies an unfinished skill transaction and explicitly permits recovery despite failed status. Run global-recover --profile <profile> with verified candidate CLI. It verifies the journal and restores previous skills under the old active pointer or confirms the already active new release. Repeat global-status with CLI compatible with the actual release. Only a passed check confirms state and permits continuing. User edits or candidate/recover errors stop dependent actions. Preserve the error/journal; do not remove it or manually overwrite skills/pointer.

Project instructions are not rewritten automatically. Apply [compatibility](../references/tools/diagnostics.md#project-compatibility): schema and meaning separately, preserving accepted exceptions. At the next project entry, check compatibility. Use highgrade-clear for a complex conflict. Report source and actual release, check result, error and pending cleanup if any. Do not imply project acceptance.

Global update does not migrate project specifications. Before reading/writing, verify CLI support for the actual catalog format and fields, including v4 and additions without a format increment. An incompatible former CLI must not edit the catalog or historical store. Migration is a separately authorized project action under the [specification reference](../references/tools/specifications.md#catalog-migration-and-recovery), with its preservation rules. Switching global CLI does not revert project formats/fields. Check preservation and catalog access separately.

<a id="совместимость-проектного-запуска-ui"></a>

## Project UI launcher compatibility

Protocol-1 launchers read the home profile active.json (schema 3) on each run, call global-status, then ui --root. Do not pin release IDs or scan projects during Update. Preview checks candidate ui --check true including launcher_protocol=1. Installed candidate repeats this before success. Unsupported protocol stops the update. Postcheck failure follows normal previous-release recovery. The same launchers then select the restored pointer. This adds no rollback after successful update. Launcher format changes need an explicit compatible migration without overwriting user files. Use isolated profiles/projects for technical checks; do not launch the actual project UI without a request.
